#uuid "5a834bf3-3872-5b5d-969c-61edac983b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374147} {:answer/id #uuid "775b3096-ba1d-5165-b372-dbf63725687c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374148} {:answer/id #uuid "03c2babe-4839-5bdc-afc4-6749f697b54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374149} {:answer/id #uuid "42f92fc3-0b2e-5275-897a-62f665a73321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374150} {:answer/id #uuid "fe97e990-5c5d-5501-b58a-2aaf7be8b50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775b3096-ba1d-5165-b372-dbf63725687c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374148} :answer-field/id #uuid "6efacc92-066c-57b7-8f28-3ccd8c8edf81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51e25390-3ec9-57e6-8340-3c34fe595d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-162149" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le 2y+z\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le y-2z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le 2y+z\\le 1,\\,1\\le y-2z\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y+z,\\,w=y-2z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{5}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [w]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881b0dd0-01c6-5013-9685-0e58c6da71c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197661} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a11a8a-6503-5229-93d1-630340faec00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197662} {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} {:answer/id #uuid "3404d280-8cad-5644-8ec1-3e6e08824288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197664} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd7243b-48c8-50fa-aaa2-6aa1ef439e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd3b3e1-98ae-532b-94b3-c5b9b8ab641b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197660}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b45928b-ff82-5faf-a3d8-f16998390abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39060" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2d1ca3-fd9b-57f2-ac29-4b16c135420c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374154} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fd9c37-4c27-5746-b851-f7b4512dd4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374155} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9008f-7ebb-5f0c-9ef4-3aba8c68542d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374156} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d1a911-155e-5064-989c-bdd887a93726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374157} {:answer/id #uuid "730b3a0c-ab79-5dd0-b3b4-eb287d28f6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9008f-7ebb-5f0c-9ef4-3aba8c68542d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374156} :answer-field/id #uuid "5287f396-f7c5-5f63-8e16-6e1738da83ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b20c6475-5823-5b74-865d-949b70ed5189" :question/math-academy-id "q-162155" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le xz\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+{x}^{2},\\,w=xz.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\le v\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{3}^{10}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}du\\cdot {\\int }_{3}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{u=1/2}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=3}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (10-3)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74cbe566-120c-5aa2-9d0f-8b0d51b92825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374161} {:answer/id #uuid "372e9f40-cc59-532c-a782-6948b90925c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374162} {:answer/id #uuid "8a16bd5e-23ba-5f37-8518-594f3a0cc660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374163} {:answer/id #uuid "455911a2-33b2-56a3-a5e6-863976f27387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374164} {:answer/id #uuid "fe23f6e2-faba-5fcc-a1dc-a65a5472035a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a16bd5e-23ba-5f37-8518-594f3a0cc660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374163} :answer-field/id #uuid "081961bd-b52b-565f-b46b-523fd43cde20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374160}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f54bd34-6966-5956-b7d3-23078157be35" :question/math-academy-id "q-162174" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1020cd0b-a734-523c-a873-ae4ccc88f60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374168} {:answer/id #uuid "eded55eb-08dc-5eea-9038-37f16794d4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374169} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6e2d78-660c-5283-ab55-0c4291532b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374170} {:answer/id #uuid "9479d209-1358-5b6d-b729-5097d39ca7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374171} {:answer/id #uuid "db3a4d38-29e5-54a0-b850-59abf8d19cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a6e2d78-660c-5283-ab55-0c4291532b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374170} :answer-field/id #uuid "11699b7d-5958-5ded-80ec-84552796d392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374167}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0695fc2-3499-5ac5-90f7-7e4077884682" :question/math-academy-id "q-162212" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-5\\le x+z\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le y\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le y-z\\le 6\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-5\\le x+z\\le 5,,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+z,\\,v=y,\\,w=y-z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-5\\le u\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{\\int }_{0}^{6}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{6}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-5}^{u=5}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=6} \\\\  & =(5-(-5))\\cdot (10-0)\\cdot (6-0) \\\\  & =600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedf6411-1c50-521c-9753-b78164606edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374175} {:answer/id #uuid "2156f8ec-e04e-51b8-bc96-f4f57ab9bd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374176} {:answer/id #uuid "641c62ce-a323-535d-adec-144d3026c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374177} {:answer/id #uuid "08e58611-c6b0-57ca-af8a-daaeefda2147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374178} {:answer/id #uuid "652a7c70-07d1-5457-a185-ceed917c21bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2156f8ec-e04e-51b8-bc96-f4f57ab9bd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374176} :answer-field/id #uuid "230795bb-1c46-5274-8505-2f18821e3031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374174}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df52d1f2-fb75-5e2b-acc7-74be8b4ade91" :question/math-academy-id "q-162213" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v={x}^{2}-y-z,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-2w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-2w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-2}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [{w}^{2}]_{w=1}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(2-(-2))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (16-1) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b43c30d-dbbd-56ae-bb9d-ec705a2a3b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481795} {:answer/id #uuid "0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481803} {:answer/id #uuid "11f2f0e2-ee85-5dc7-8bf8-e4bbb3c1a106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481822} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ad71c3-d535-5d86-9412-6ac3d6c65116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482325} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d483b0-65b2-53a0-9add-ad98d8a18577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481803} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f66c5c-6087-5c29-8319-6c31eb512359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482500}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162183" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c637b6f-4f06-51ca-9616-b193dbfe0073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481798} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb8c5ce-8dbf-5386-8235-8248286f77f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481846} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab67b62-04e2-59bc-825a-dfe5cf1d691d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481954} {:answer/id #uuid "547236d6-bb1e-5d75-97ea-1f9bb0063831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481977} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482297} :answer-field/id #uuid "694f0a99-bbd9-58dc-bc56-6c2fef66f7a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482432}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162211" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42bc746b-b715-55f2-86ec-fd72bb4f4ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481937} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8522ed-fdef-5b29-b95a-c55a7f972e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481957} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482276} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5075d2-9ee4-5c53-a21e-1de9889cd0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482299} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7b48d0-b052-528a-ae87-b2b5f4f33257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482276} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d2256b3-469f-5f0e-a313-ab48af9bfa87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162153" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481843} {:answer/id #uuid "24ed6cbd-b045-57ee-bdcf-da55c1ab6bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481871} {:answer/id #uuid "6b962412-6d6a-5fe6-8c93-21ebb16264be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482021} {:answer/id #uuid "6c34f66d-0108-524b-a0bb-ea16f3872c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482024} {:answer/id #uuid "b92bb4be-1ea9-57c7-8327-65aa672b6f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481843} :answer-field/id #uuid "80cdcf0b-fbd2-5ef6-980d-ca6cc3566902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482447}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162170" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12666955-acda-50b1-a759-415719031788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498766} {:answer/id #uuid "33260567-19af-5f87-8391-f7b7b20532c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498847} {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} {:answer/id #uuid "c17ed11d-1615-505b-9ee3-f62b086faf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499157} {:answer/id #uuid "faf10f5c-fe15-5f32-843c-38243f61e787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} :answer-field/id #uuid "772a5b09-0846-503c-9495-bc958a42c8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499375}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92990ecc-5ba0-56e3-944d-6e84f299eb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-162210" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-{e}^{x},\\,v=y,\\,w=z{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & 0 & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040577a1-f488-586f-a244-5ac52452a051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498738} {:answer/id #uuid "134f7fbf-b645-5f5e-a680-2cf1d5fc853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498772} {:answer/id #uuid "7649b34b-2218-533c-b3c5-146847fddc3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498997} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} {:answer/id #uuid "e83877aa-f978-5e05-a20d-0069c96a2685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} :answer-field/id #uuid "612abc3f-006a-54a9-ba2c-ed70e2955dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499358}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad40770e-3352-5877-ade7-ccfd965beccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162185" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-y,\\,v=x,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56aff325-7b07-5275-8c12-1b2e8759ff4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545085} {:answer/id #uuid "6add7eb0-9ea7-5148-a402-b2bdc51fbb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545091} {:answer/id #uuid "792b7082-6807-5196-9601-1d26374888cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545095} {:answer/id #uuid "adaa1cc7-706e-598c-b2ec-5be249f3d877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545099} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc345c3-aeee-5369-a84c-30e8f9cb2d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6add7eb0-9ea7-5148-a402-b2bdc51fbb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2236ae3-1e28-597d-a5b3-9b0419cdd9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545107}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07e55509-b25a-54e5-8c3a-f3a8ce4459c4" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le - 3 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 3 z\\le 3\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le - 3 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 3 z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=- 3 x + y,\\quad w=3 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 3\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},\\frac{3 u}{2} + v,\\frac{w}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{3}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3fce2d-b0fb-5de7-956f-6f1597c9fe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545078} {:answer/id #uuid "463e4f8d-3236-5dea-988d-12549f60bab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545083} {:answer/id #uuid "6f72fe8e-20ee-5bf9-8652-252c8f94140c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545092} {:answer/id #uuid "74a2023b-70f4-5988-b24e-a88339e0fa2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545093} {:answer/id #uuid "e19c619e-1028-574d-92ee-f6105319a63d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3fce2d-b0fb-5de7-956f-6f1597c9fe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545078} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb445c12-48c4-530f-b2ed-dc019c34045f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545109}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c37e2fc-f4eb-5061-a3a1-4d88ea3a6767" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 4\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=2 x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 4\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},- u + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2f499d-3b71-54c8-878c-df1526bd355e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545087} {:answer/id #uuid "671f0fae-58af-5409-b296-def7ac5666b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545089} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa7b22e-94ea-562c-97e1-b8f1143c580d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545090} {:answer/id #uuid "a8dd97d3-4024-52d7-9d5c-689d01e28958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545097} {:answer/id #uuid "d840e2e5-8bba-581d-994e-de4f7cacc8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c2f499d-3b71-54c8-878c-df1526bd355e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545087} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0da8d7-797d-527f-b2d1-fa1fc9fbc734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545086}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acb9920a-c4c3-5163-83aa-da5aa673fe37" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 3 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 3 x\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x,\\quad v=x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{3},- \\frac{u}{3} + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{4}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b358110-f098-5b00-b994-090f1909a25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545076} {:answer/id #uuid "51890235-6f06-5902-9890-b9a7fdb916d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545084} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6cd16e-dd2d-5a8f-b2ca-b029c56b79b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545088} {:answer/id #uuid "7511abe9-750b-5ffd-b849-345cffb8b183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545094} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ee32e-d4e4-5d12-86b8-17197e316325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6cd16e-dd2d-5a8f-b2ca-b029c56b79b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545088} :answer-field/id #uuid "15249b49-ff93-5f2c-93db-24c193e0cd42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545079}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7d71c23-13c3-5551-bd04-90d1004e74ff" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(x,y,z)\\, :\\, 0\\le 2 x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le - 2 x + y\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le 2 x\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le - 2 x + y\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le 2 z\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x,\\quad v=- 2 x + y,\\quad w=2 z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto(u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\big\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 6,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 5,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(0\\right)(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nThe map to $R$ is\n\n$$\nT(u,v,w)=\\left(\\frac{u}{2},u + v,\\frac{w}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta 1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{6}\\int_{0}^{5}\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\int_{0}^{6}1\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\cdot \\int_{0}^{5}1\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}1\\,\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{6}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{5}\\cdot [w]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(5\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #